{"from": "site:bethany-beyond-jordan", "type": "attests", "to": "place:jordan-river", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated baptism site with its mikveh pools lies on the east bank of the Jordan River - the river valley where John preached and where the 'reed shaken by the wind' is spoken."}
{"from": "site:bethany-beyond-jordan", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-al-maghtas"}
{"from": "artifact:chorazin-olive-millstones", "type": "attests", "to": "place:chorazin", "grade": "C", "note": "Millstones for olive-oil extraction from the excavated village show the economy of ancient Galilean villages like Chorazin."}
{"from": "artifact:chorazin-olive-millstones", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-11-21-chorazin", "grade": "C", "note": "The excavated village's olive-oil millstones show a real basalt settlement at the traditional site; they do not date the 1st-century town."}
{"from": "artifact:chorazin-olive-millstones", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-chorazin-millstones"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-bethsaida", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-11-21-bethsaida", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus names the village 'Bethsaids, situate at the lake of Gennesareth', advanced by Philip to the dignity of a city and renamed Julias - the same town at the same lake in the first century."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-bethsaida", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-ant18-bethsaida"}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-5-asphaltites", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-11-23-24-sodom-judgment", "grade": "C", "note": "Pliny describes the Asphaltites (Dead Sea), the region of the Sodom memory, as producing 'nothing whatever except bitumen' with water in which 'the bodies of animals will not sink' - the setting of the judgment tradition, not the judgment itself."}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-5-asphaltites", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:perseus-pliny-5-asphaltites"}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-5-tyre-sidon", "type": "attests", "to": "place:tyre", "grade": "A", "note": "Pliny, writing in the first century, describes Tyre as a standing city so famous in ancient times, its fame now confined to the murex and purple trade. Regraded B to A by the architect (2026-11-14): a classical author naming the place is A for the place, the precedent applied since Matthew 11."}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-5-tyre-sidon", "type": "attests", "to": "place:sidon", "grade": "B", "note": "Pliny describes Sidon, famous for its manufacture of glass, as a standing first-century town."}
{"from": "site:chorazin-synagogue", "type": "attests", "to": "place:chorazin", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated basalt village with a synagogue at its centre, on the Korazim Plateau 2.5 mi from Capernaum; the standing synagogue is late 3rd century."}
